# The committed ./word binary is tracked: it's the reproducible seed (see
# docs/BOOTSTRAP.md). Ignore only local build byproducts.

# object files from checking the encoder against GNU `as` by hand
# (dev/toolchain/test_encoder_vs_as.sh keeps its own in a temp directory)
*.o

# Python bytecode, written to bootstrap/__pycache__ when you run the Python
# bootstrap (bootstrap/wordc.py, as test_bootstrap_seed.sh does)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# benchmark byproducts: bench.sh compiles the timing helper when it's missing,
# and jsonbench/gen.w generates the JSON input document
dev/benchmarks/runstat
dev/benchmarks/jsonbench/data.json

# program output: examples/rpg writes its save file in whatever directory it's
# run from, and dev/benchmarks/jsonbench/gen.w writes data.json the same way.
# Running either from the repo root puts the file here.
/crypt-journal.json
/data.json

# `word build dev/benchmarks/vecmath/vecmath.w` with no -o, run from here
/vecmath

# The Windows binary is a local cross-build, not a second committed seed:
# `word build -win compiler/word.w -o word.exe` at the root makes it, and the
# release workflow builds its own. Only the Linux `word` above is the auditable
# seed (docs/BOOTSTRAP.md).
/word.exe

# release artifacts: .github/workflows/release.yml cross-builds the four targets
# into dist/ and writes its release notes beside them
/dist/
/notes.md

# local packaging byproducts. Nothing in the repo makes these (the release
# workflow builds its own into dist/), but it's easy to pack the tree at the
# root by hand to see what an archive would hold, and a `git add -A` once
# committed one.
/word.tar
/word.zip
